  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CHAI - Increasing Skilled Birth Attendants inRural Zambia - Increasing Skilled Birth Attendants in  Zambia</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">CHAI- Increasing Skilled Birth Attendants in Rural Zambia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project to increase the number and quality of health peronnel in rural areas of Zambia, with the overall goal of contributing to reduction of morbidity and mortality in women and their newborn babies. The project also aims to strengthen GRZ systems for training  supportive supervision and mentorship and collect evidenced on retention strategies for health personnel in rural settings</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Projektets syfte &#xe4;r att &#xf6;ka antalet sjukv&#xe5;rdare, och kvalit&#xe9;n av sjukv&#xe5;rden, p&#xe5; zambiska landsbygden. M&#xe5;let &#xe4;r att bidra till att minska oh&#xe4;lsa och d&#xf6;dsfall bland kvinnor och nyf&#xf6;dda. Projektet hoppas &#xe4;ven bidra till att p&#xe5; olika s&#xe4;tt st&#xe4;rka GRZ system f&#xf6;r att f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttra tillg&#xe5;ng till, och kvalit&#xe9;n av, sjukv&#xe5;rden p&#xe5; landsbygden (bl.a. genom utbildning av personal, insamling av data och mentorskapsprogram).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overall outcome of this intervention was to increase the number of quality Skilled Birth Attendants (SBA&apos;s) deployed and accessible in rural districts of Zambia,following a serious human resources for health crisis in preceding years. The SBAs health service provision is expected to increase use of preventive and curative maternal and newborn health care, with a positive health impact for the rural Zambian population.This was aimed at addressing the existing health care worker shortage which was a contributory factor to the&#xa0; high maternal and neonatal mortality rates in Zambia.
The main objectives were:
1) Support the Ministry of Health (MoH) to develop and strengthen systems to train and enable 3,732 new &#x2018;mid-level&#x2019; SBAs to provide quality preventive and curative maternal and newborn health care. It was expected that a minimum of 30% of students would be recruited from and subsequently deployed back to their rural communities.2) Strengthen Government of Zambia systems for supportive supervision and clinical mentorship of SBAs post-graduation in 14 districts across Northern, Muchinga and Luapula Provinces, and collect evidence to inform government policy decisions with regards to scaling up the system nationally.3) Collect evidence on retention strategies that could be successful in retaining health workers in rural areas across Zambia. The evidence collected would be shared with the Zambian Government to inform policy decisions around health worker retention strategies.</narrative>
  </description>
